{#
	The timeline, in the two places it is read: a card on the record and a page of
	its own (XIV-3).

	One entry is one line — when, what, who, and how many things it touched — with
	the changes themselves behind a native <details>. Native, not a Bootstrap
	collapse: it needs no JavaScript, it is open to find-in-page in some browsers,
	and a timeline is exactly the kind of thing somebody reads with the keyboard.

	Both callers import this, so the compact row and the detail under it are
	described once. Before this the record page carried the whole thing inline and
	every entry showed every change, which is what made fifty of them unreadable
	and five hundred of them a wall.
#}

{#
	One stored value, rendered as the field it describes.

	History keeps values in storage form and carries the label it had at the time
	(§5.2), so an entry stays readable after the field is renamed — and after it is
	deleted, when there is no definition left to ask. That is the fallback here:
	the type formats it when the field still exists, and the raw stored value shows
	when it does not.
#}
{% macro value(shape, key, stored) %}
	{%- set field = shape ? shape.getField(key) : null -%}
	{%- if stored is null -%}
		<span class="text-body-secondary">empty</span>
	{%- elseif field -%}
		{{ display_stored(field, stored) }}
	{%- else -%}
		{{ stored }}
	{%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}

{# What changed, listed. Only ever rendered inside an entry that has some. #}
{% macro detail(module, entry) %}
	{% import _self as h %}
	<ul class="text-body-secondary small mt-2 mb-0 ps-3">
		{# Ordered by the definitions, because jsonb does not keep the order these
		   were written in. #}
		{% for key, change in in_field_order(module, entry.fieldChanges) %}
			<li>
				{{ change.label }}:
				{{ h.value(module, key, change.from) }} → {{ h.value(module, key, change.to) }}
			</li>
		{% endfor %}

		{% for collectionKey, rows in entry.collectionChanges %}
			{% set collection = module.getCollection(collectionKey) %}
			{% for row in rows %}
				<li>
					{{ collection ? collection.label : collectionKey }}
					{{ ('module.row_action.' ~ row.action)|trans }}:
					{% if row.action == 'updated' %}
						{% for key, change in in_field_order(collection, row.changes) %}
							{{ change.label }}
							{{ h.value(collection, key, change.from) }} → {{ h.value(collection, key, change.to) }}{{ loop.last ? '' : ',' }}
						{% endfor %}
					{% else %}
						{% for key, held in in_field_order(collection, row.values) %}
							{{ h.value(collection, key, held.value) }}{{ loop.last ? '' : ',' }}
						{% endfor %}
					{% endif %}
				</li>
			{% endfor %}
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
{% endmacro %}

{#
	One entry, compact: a line you scan, and a detail you open.

	The line is built once and rendered inside a <summary> or a plain div, so an
	entry with nothing to show — a record being created — reads the same as one
	that opens, minus the marker.
#}
{% macro entry(module, entry) %}
	{% import _self as h %}

	{% set line %}
		<time class="text-body-secondary font-monospace" datetime="{{ entry.occurredAt|date('c') }}">
			{{- entry.occurredAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') -}}
		</time>
		{#
			The verb is a key rather than the stored value capitalised: "updated" is
			English, and the timeline is read by whoever owns the record (XIV-8).
			Nobody signed in is an import or a console command, not a gap.
		#}
		<strong class="ms-2">{{ ('module.action.' ~ entry.action.value)|trans }}</strong>
		<span class="text-body-secondary">
			{{- ' ' }}{{ 'module.action_by'|trans({'%who%': entry.userLabel ?? ('module.action_by_system'|trans)}) -}}
		</span>
		{% if entry.hasDetail %}
			<span class="badge text-bg-light fw-normal ms-1">
				{{- 'module.history_changes'|trans({'%count%': entry.changeCount}) -}}
			</span>
		{% elseif entry.document %}
			{# An entry that changed nothing still has something to say: which
			   template, and which format came out of it (XIV-4). #}
			<span class="badge text-bg-light fw-normal ms-1">
				{{- entry.document.template }} · {{ entry.document.format|upper -}}
			</span>
		{% elseif entry.email %}
			{#
				A mail that left the building, or one that tried to (XIV-39).
				*Whether* it went is the verb above and not repeated here — a
				timeline is scanned down its left-hand column, and a failure that
				only announced itself in the badge would be a failure somebody
				reads past. What the badge adds is the part the verb cannot: which
				template, and which address it was sent to, both as they were at
				the time rather than as the contact reads today.
			#}
			<span class="badge text-bg-light fw-normal ms-1">
				{{- entry.email.template }} · {{ entry.email.recipient -}}
			</span>
			{#
				What went with it, where something did (XIV-40). One entry, not two:
				the document was made in order to be attached, so it is a property of
				this act rather than an act of its own — and on a `email_failed` row
				its presence is the sentence "the invoice was made and the mail
				server refused it", which is a different problem from a document that
				could not be produced at all.
			#}
			{% set attached = entry.email.attachment|default(null) %}
			{% if attached %}
				<span class="badge text-bg-light fw-normal ms-1">
					<i class="bi bi-paperclip" aria-hidden="true"></i>
					{{ attached.template }} · {{ attached.format|upper }}
				</span>
			{% endif %}
			<span class="text-body-secondary">{{ entry.email.subject }}</span>
		{% endif %}
	{% endset %}

	{% if entry.hasDetail %}
		<details class="history-entry">
			<summary class="d-block">{{ line }}</summary>
			{{ h.detail(module, entry) }}
		</details>
	{% else %}
		{# Aligned with the ones that open, so a column of times stays a column. #}
		<div class="history-entry history-entry-plain">{{ line }}</div>
	{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{# A list of entries, one line each. #}
{% macro entries(module, list) %}
	{% import _self as h %}
	<ul class="list-unstyled small mb-0">
		{% for entry in list %}
			<li class="py-1 {{ loop.first ? '' : 'border-top' }}">{{ h.entry(module, entry) }}</li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
{% endmacro %}
